item.content:encoded:
A few people were surprised by how low you can go with NixOS, so i thought I would do a write up as I did not realise this was anything special, I kept notes when doing it but not for the intention of writing a blog article so i might miss small tweaks I did here and there.I initially set aside half a day for this; it actually took closer to 3 days.This guide is not very focused on saving disk space. If you want a disk space saving guide that has already been done by someone else here: https://nixcademy.com/posts/minimizing-nixos-images/ this has its own set of challenges and drawbacks so if you want to layer them together, be my guest  I have decided not to implement the disk-minimising options into the TierHive NixOS template as I feel it is too likely to cause a default that end users are unlikely to expect.Stock deployment before any changes and with cloud-init, it runs on 1024MB RAM, JUST!, it sat at 288MB used with 89 kernel modules loaded, and a plain same-release rebuild needed 896MB (at 768 nix-build got OOM-killed at 505MB anon).It now idles at 96MB used on a 512MB VPS with 54 modules, and a full channel update plus rebuild, including a kernel switch to 7.3 if you want to do that, It finishes at 512MB with a 371MB peak and no OOM. All numbers are from my own runs sometimes I recorded exact numbers others I left out the KB's and only recorded the MB's  , measured a few minutes after the second boot with nobody logged in using my own build environment testing tools (I never log in to templates before use so they are clean)Roughly in the order and things done on this template (even the harmless fails):sysctl.vm.mem_profiling=never on the cmdline. The NixOS kernel ships with allocation profiling enabled by default and the tracking tables cost about 6MB (3.9MB vmalloc, 1.9MB slab). the bare  which is left in isn't a valid parameter; the kernel juts hands it to userspace and nothing changes, it needs the  prefix. gets back the 2MB KFENCE pool. and  get rid of the kdamond and kauditd threads.initcall_blacklist=ksm_init,tpm_init,init_tis drops ksmd (1.4MB) and the TPM bits (0.3MB plus a kthread). No TPM in these guests and KSM inside a guest is pointless. (For TierHive anyway)psi=0 ras=cec_disable nowatchdog nmi_watchdog=0 cryptomgr.notests 8250.nr_uarts=1 are all small, mostly fewer kthreads and less noise. On a 2GB test VM the whole cmd line set took non-cache memory from 128.0 to 121MB and kthreads from 64 to 59.transparent_hugepage=never was one of the biggest single wins. With THP on madvise the kernel bumps  to 41108 (3710 without THP at 896MB), which is reserved memory you never get to use. Turning it off freed 52MB at 2GB and gave 103MB more available at 896MB, obviously in high end or production there are reasons not to do this, but  day to day use, you probably wont notice any difference.Blacklisted the hardware a KVM guest never has or doesn't need: floppy, intel_agp, i2c_piix4, psmouse, mousedev, joydev, serio_raw, evdev, button (using tiny_power_button already handles ACPI shutdown), the rapl/cstate/uncore lot, kvm, cfg80211 (networkd was pulling it in for nl80211), ata_generic/pata_acpi, qemu_fw_cfg, dmi_sysfs, vga16fb.Blacklisted virtio_console as well, nothing uses the agent channel and it was holding an 8MB kmalloc-4k slab.vsock, vmci and virtiofs get  rather than a blacklist. They get requested at switch-root with no device present, and with a plain blacklist vmw_vmci just loaded instead.Blacklisted virtio_gpu and let the VNC console run on plain vgacon through virtio-vga. That one was really for a 6.18.59 fix/workaround, it does not follow to 7.3 but thats optional I guess.Initrd cut down to virtio_pci virtio_blk ext4 with includeDefaultModules = false, without importing the qemu-guest profile.Trimmed the kernel module tree with a small derivation that replaces . It drops sound, GPU, wifi, USB, physical NIC, SCSI/ATA HBA drivers and so on, always keeping the deps of anything it keeps. 162MB down to 45MB on disk, and udev has nothing silly left to autoload. It runs inside  so kernel updates get the same treatment for free.Disabled systemd-resolved, 8.3MB PSS and it was listening for LLMNR/mDNS on 0.0.0.0:5355. cloud-init writes a plain resolv.conf instead.Swapped systemd-timesyncd (4.6MB) for a oneshot  on a timer, at boot and hourly, so nothing stays resident (won't work everywhere but on most KVM hosts it should be fine)services.openssh.startWhenNeeded = true so sshd is socket activated and the 3.9MB listener only exists while someone is connected. (Perfect for NAT but if you are sitting with port 22 listening for connections on a public IP you might want to skip this)Disabled systemd-oomd (1.8MB).A tiny oneshot drops  into the cloud-init generated .network files, which stops the networkd LLDP socket and af_packet loading at idle.What's left resident is systemd, udevd, nsncd, networkd, journald, logind, dbus-broker and one getty, about 40MB of userland PSS.zram with zstd at  capped at 512MB, no disk swap. This, plus the next one, is what makes rebuilding in 512MB possible. so the kernel would rather compress cold anon pages into zram than throw away page cache,  since read-ahead on zram is wasted memory, and vm.watermark_scale_factor = 125 with vm.watermark_boost_factor = 0 so kswapd starts earlier and the rebuild doesn't stall in direct reclaim. During a rebuild at 512MB zram peaked at 116MB of real RAM.Tuned the Boehm GC that Nix uses, set system wide with  so  picks it up. Evaluating the module system On a 2GB VM nix-instantiate '<nixpkgs/nixos>' -A system peaked at 599MB stock (Nix 2.34 pre-grows its heap to 25% of RAM, capped at 384MB). GC_INITIAL_HEAP_SIZE=33554432 took it to 597, adding  to 562, adding GC_FORCE_UNMAP_ON_GCOLLECT=1 to 548MB. I couldn't find anything else that meaningfully moves eval memory.Trimmed the closure, which is mostly disk but means less to pull and manage on update: environment.defaultPackages = [ ], documentation off, xdg mime/icons/sounds off, fontconfig off, lvm and bcache off, systemd-importd suppressed (it drags in gnupg, openldap and cyrus-sasl, about 18MB), nixos-option off (man-db and groff, about 11MB), nixos-install/enter/build-vms off, and  forced off to lose xauth. Closure went from 963MB to 910MB and the compressed image from 607MB to 477MB.The one thing 512MB still struggles with is jumping releases (26.05 to unstable). On a 4GB disk the first attempt went on for about 30 minutes before nix-build got OOM-killed, the old generation kept running fine, and running it again finished because everything was already downloaded. If you're doing a release jump give it more RAM for the duration or expect to run it twice and dont bother waiting 30 minutes, just kill it after 5/10 and run it a second time, that said this was to a pre release as thats all I could find to jump too so maybe that had something to do with it.Full config below. Some of tierhive.nix is platform stuff (cloud-init, password resets, reserved blocks) rather than RAM related, but it's the complete working files.{ config, lib, pkgs, ... }:

{
  imports = [
    ./hardware-configuration.nix
    ./tierhive.nix
  ];

  environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [ curl rsync ];

  system.stateVersion = "26.05";
}
hardware-configuration.nix{ config, lib, pkgs, modulesPath, ... }:

{
  boot.initrd.includeDefaultModules = false;
  boot.initrd.availableKernelModules = [ "virtio_pci" "virtio_blk" "ext4" ];
  boot.initrd.kernelModules = [ ];
  boot.kernelModules = [ ];
  boot.extraModulePackages = [ ];

  fileSystems."/" = {
    device = "/dev/disk/by-label/nixos";
    fsType = "ext4";
  };

  swapDevices = [ ];

  nixpkgs.hostPlatform = lib.mkDefault "x86_64-linux";
}
# TierHive VPS platform settings: network, DNS and hostname come from the panel via cloud-init on first boot.
{ config, lib, pkgs, ... }:

let
  # Kernel module tree without drivers for hardware a KVM VPS never has; kept modules always keep their dependencies.
  kernel = config.boot.kernelPackages.kernel;
  moduleTreeRemove = [
    "kernel/sound/*"
    "kernel/drivers/accel/*" "kernel/drivers/accessibility/*" "kernel/drivers/atm/*" "kernel/drivers/auxdisplay/*" "kernel/drivers/bcma/*"
    "kernel/drivers/bluetooth/*" "kernel/drivers/bus/*" "kernel/drivers/clk/*" "kernel/drivers/comedi/*" "kernel/drivers/counter/*"
    "kernel/drivers/cpufreq/*" "kernel/drivers/crypto/*" "kernel/drivers/cxl/*" "kernel/drivers/dca/*" "kernel/drivers/devfreq/*"
    "kernel/drivers/dma/*" "kernel/drivers/edac/*" "kernel/drivers/extcon/*" "kernel/drivers/firewire/*" "kernel/drivers/fpga/*"
    "kernel/drivers/fsi/*" "kernel/drivers/fwctl/*" "kernel/drivers/gnss/*" "kernel/drivers/gpio/*" "kernel/drivers/greybus/*"
    "kernel/drivers/hid/*" "kernel/drivers/hsi/*" "kernel/drivers/hv/*" "kernel/drivers/hwmon/*" "kernel/drivers/hwtracing/*"
    "kernel/drivers/i2c/*" "kernel/drivers/i3c/*" "kernel/drivers/iio/*" "kernel/drivers/infiniband/hw/*" "kernel/drivers/ipack/*"
    "kernel/drivers/leds/*" "kernel/drivers/macintosh/*" "kernel/drivers/mailbox/*" "kernel/drivers/mcb/*" "kernel/drivers/media/*"
    "kernel/drivers/memstick/*" "kernel/drivers/message/*" "kernel/drivers/mfd/*" "kernel/drivers/mmc/*" "kernel/drivers/most/*"
    "kernel/drivers/mtd/*" "kernel/drivers/mux/*" "kernel/drivers/nfc/*" "kernel/drivers/ntb/*" "kernel/drivers/nvdimm/*"
    "kernel/drivers/nvmem/*" "kernel/drivers/parport/*" "kernel/drivers/pcmcia/*" "kernel/drivers/peci/*" "kernel/drivers/perf/*"
    "kernel/drivers/phy/*" "kernel/drivers/pinctrl/*" "kernel/drivers/platform/*" "kernel/drivers/power/*" "kernel/drivers/powercap/*"
    "kernel/drivers/rapidio/*" "kernel/drivers/ras/*" "kernel/drivers/regulator/*" "kernel/drivers/reset/*" "kernel/drivers/rpmsg/*"
    "kernel/drivers/siox/*" "kernel/drivers/slimbus/*" "kernel/drivers/soc/*" "kernel/drivers/soundwire/*" "kernel/drivers/spi/*"
    "kernel/drivers/spmi/*" "kernel/drivers/ssb/*" "kernel/drivers/staging/*" "kernel/drivers/tee/*" "kernel/drivers/thermal/*"
    "kernel/drivers/thunderbolt/*" "kernel/drivers/ufs/*" "kernel/drivers/usb/*" "kernel/drivers/w1/*" "kernel/drivers/xen/*"
    "kernel/drivers/video/fbdev/*" "kernel/drivers/video/backlight/*" "kernel/drivers/gpu/drm/*/*"
    "kernel/drivers/input/joystick/*" "kernel/drivers/input/touchscreen/*" "kernel/drivers/input/tablet/*"
    "kernel/drivers/input/gameport/*" "kernel/drivers/input/rmi4/*"
    "kernel/drivers/net/ethernet/*" "kernel/drivers/net/wireless/*" "kernel/drivers/net/wwan/*" "kernel/drivers/net/usb/*"
    "kernel/drivers/net/arcnet/*" "kernel/drivers/net/fddi/*" "kernel/drivers/net/hamradio/*" "kernel/drivers/net/hippi/*"
    "kernel/drivers/net/ieee802154/*" "kernel/drivers/net/wan/*" "kernel/drivers/net/plip/*" "kernel/drivers/net/fjes/*"
    "kernel/drivers/net/hyperv/*" "kernel/drivers/net/vmxnet3/*" "kernel/drivers/net/xen-netback/*" "kernel/drivers/net/xen-netfront.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/net/thunderbolt/*" "kernel/drivers/net/mhi_net.ko*" "kernel/drivers/net/dsa/*" "kernel/drivers/net/rionet.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/net/ntb_netdev.ko*" "kernel/drivers/net/sungem_phy.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/block/floppy.ko*" "kernel/drivers/block/mtip32xx/*" "kernel/drivers/block/xen-blk*"
    "kernel/drivers/scsi/*" "kernel/drivers/ata/*" "kernel/drivers/watchdog/*" "kernel/drivers/rtc/*"
  ];
  moduleTreeKeep = [
    "kernel/drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/*"
    "kernel/drivers/scsi/scsi_mod.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/scsi_common.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/sd_mod.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/sr_mod.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/scsi/sg.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/st.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/ch.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/ses.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/scsi/raid_class.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/virtio_scsi.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/scsi_transport_*"
    "kernel/drivers/scsi/iscsi_tcp.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/libiscsi.ko*" "kernel/drivers/scsi/libiscsi_tcp.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/scsi/iscsi_boot_sysfs.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/ata/libata.ko*" "kernel/drivers/ata/ata_piix.ko*" "kernel/drivers/ata/ahci.ko*" "kernel/drivers/ata/libahci.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/watchdog/watchdog.ko*" "kernel/drivers/watchdog/i6300esb.ko*" "kernel/drivers/watchdog/softdog.ko*"
    "kernel/drivers/rtc/rtc-cmos.ko*"
  ];
  modulesSrc = lib.getOutput "modules" kernel;
  trimmedModules = pkgs.runCommand "${kernel.name}-modules-tierhive" { nativeBuildInputs = [ pkgs.kmod ]; } ''
    set -f
    remove="${lib.concatStringsSep " " moduleTreeRemove}"
    keep="${lib.concatStringsSep " " moduleTreeKeep}"
    ver=$(ls ${modulesSrc}/lib/modules)
    src=${modulesSrc}/lib/modules/$ver
    dst=$out/lib/modules/$ver
    match() { local f=$1 p; for p in $2; do case $f in $p) return 0;; esac; done; return 1; }
    declare -A want
    while IFS= read -r line; do
      m=''${line%%:*}
      if match "$m" "$remove" && ! match "$m" "$keep"; then continue; fi
      want[$m]=1
      for d in ''${line#*:}; do want[$d]=1; done
    done < $src/modules.dep
    for m in "''${!want[@]}"; do mkdir -p "$dst/$(dirname "$m")"; cp "$src/$m" "$dst/$m"; done
    cp $src/modules.builtin $src/modules.builtin.modinfo $dst/
    while IFS= read -r o; do
      for m in "$o" "$o.xz" "$o.zst" "$o.gz"; do [ -n "''${want[$m]:-}" ] && { echo "$o"; break; }; done
    done < $src/modules.order > $dst/modules.order
    depmod -b $out -a $ver
  '';
in
{
  boot.loader.grub.enable = true;
  boot.loader.grub.device = "/dev/vda";

  boot.kernelParams = [
    "sysctl.vm.mem_profiling=never" "damon_stat.enabled=N" "kfence.sample_interval=0" "audit=0"
    "initcall_blacklist=ksm_init,tpm_init,init_tis" "psi=0" "ras=cec_disable" "nowatchdog" "nmi_watchdog=0"
    "cryptomgr.notests" "8250.nr_uarts=1" "transparent_hugepage=never"
  ];
  system.modulesTree = lib.mkForce ([ trimmedModules ] ++ config.boot.extraModulePackages);
  boot.blacklistedKernelModules = [
    "virtio_console" "floppy" "intel_agp" "i2c_piix4" "psmouse" "mousedev" "joydev" "serio_raw" "mac_hid"
    "input_leds" "evdev" "button" "rapl" "intel_rapl_msr" "intel_rapl_common" "intel_cstate" "intel_uncore"
    "kvm_intel" "kvm_amd" "kvm" "cfg80211" "ata_generic" "pata_acpi" "qemu_fw_cfg" "dmi_sysfs" "vga16fb" "virtio_gpu"
  ];
  boot.extraModprobeConfig = lib.concatMapStrings (m: "install ${m} ${pkgs.coreutils}/bin/false\n") [
    "vsock" "vmw_vsock_virtio_transport" "vsock_loopback" "vmw_vsock_vmci_transport" "vmw_vmci" "virtiofs"
  ];

  boot.kernel.sysctl = {
    "kernel.panic_on_oops" = 1;
    "kernel.panic" = 10;
    "vm.swappiness" = 180;
    "vm.page-cluster" = 0;
    "vm.watermark_scale_factor" = 125;
    "vm.watermark_boost_factor" = 0;
  };

  services.udev.extraRules = ''
    ACTION=="add|change", KERNEL=="vd[a-z]*", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="disk", ATTR{queue/scheduler}="none", ATTR{queue/read_ahead_kb}="128"
  '';

  zramSwap = {
    enable = true;
    algorithm = "zstd";
    memoryPercent = 100;
    memoryMax = 536870912;
  };

  networking.hostName = "";
  networking.useDHCP = false;
  networking.resolvconf.enable = false;
  services.resolved.enable = false;
  systemd.oomd.enable = false;

  services.timesyncd.enable = false;
  systemd.services.tierhive-ntp = {
    description = "One-shot clock sync";
    after = [ "network-online.target" ];
    wants = [ "network-online.target" ];
    serviceConfig.Type = "oneshot";
    serviceConfig.ExecStart = "${pkgs.busybox}/bin/ntpd -n -q ${lib.concatMapStringsSep " " (s: "-p ${s}") config.networking.timeServers}";
  };
  systemd.timers.tierhive-ntp = {
    wantedBy = [ "timers.target" ];
    timerConfig.OnBootSec = "30s";
    timerConfig.OnCalendar = "*-*-* *:17:00";
  };

  environment.etc."systemd/journald.conf.d/10-tierhive.conf".text = ''
    [Journal]
    SystemMaxUse=50M
  '';

  environment.variables = {
    GC_INITIAL_HEAP_SIZE = "33554432";
    GC_FREE_SPACE_DIVISOR = "8";
    GC_FORCE_UNMAP_ON_GCOLLECT = "1";
  };

  environment.defaultPackages = [ ];
  xdg.mime.enable = false;
  xdg.icons.enable = false;
  xdg.sounds.enable = false;
  fonts.fontconfig.enable = false;
  services.lvm.enable = false;
  boot.bcache.enable = false;
  systemd.suppressedSystemUnits = [ "systemd-importd.service" "sys-kernel-config.mount" ];
  system.tools.nixos-option.enable = false;
  system.tools.nixos-build-vms.enable = false;
  system.tools.nixos-install.enable = false;
  system.tools.nixos-enter.enable = false;
  security.pam.services.su.forwardXAuth = lib.mkForce false;

  documentation.enable = false;
  documentation.man.enable = false;
  documentation.nixos.enable = false;

  services.openssh = {
    enable = true;
    startWhenNeeded = true;
    settings.PermitRootLogin = null;
    settings.PasswordAuthentication = null;
    settings.KbdInteractiveAuthentication = false;
    extraConfig = "Include /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf";
  };
  security.pam.services.sshd.unixAuth = lib.mkForce true;
  systemd.tmpfiles.rules = [ "d /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d 0755 root root -" ];

  environment.etc.fstab.mode = "0644";

  services.cloud-init = {
    enable = true;
    network.enable = true;
    settings = {
      datasource_list = [ "NoCloud" ];
      system_info = {
        distro = "nixos";
        network.renderers = [ "networkd" ];
        paths.templates_dir = "${pkgs.cloud-init}/${pkgs.python3.sitePackages}/etc/cloud/templates";
      };
      unverified_modules = [ "resolv_conf" ];
      ssh_deletekeys = false;
      ssh_genkeytypes = [ ];
      cloud_init_modules = [ "seed_random" "bootcmd" "write_files" "growpart" "resizefs" "set_hostname" "update_hostname" "update_etc_hosts" "resolv_conf" "users_groups" "ssh" ];
      cloud_config_modules = [ "set_passwords" "runcmd" ];
      cloud_final_modules = [ "package_update_upgrade_install" "write_files_deferred" "scripts_vendor" "scripts_per_once" "scripts_per_boot" "scripts_per_instance" "scripts_user" "keys_to_console" "final_message" ];
    };
  };
  systemd.services.cloud-init-local.unitConfig.ConditionPathExists = "!/etc/cloud/cloud-init.disabled";
  systemd.services.cloud-init.unitConfig.ConditionPathExists = "!/etc/cloud/cloud-init.disabled";
  systemd.services.cloud-config.unitConfig.ConditionPathExists = "!/etc/cloud/cloud-init.disabled";
  systemd.services.cloud-final.unitConfig.ConditionPathExists = "!/etc/cloud/cloud-init.disabled";

  systemd.services.tierhive-lldp-off = {
    description = "Disable LLDP reception on the cloud-init network files";
    unitConfig.DefaultDependencies = false;
    after = [ "cloud-init-local.service" ];
    before = [ "systemd-networkd.service" ];
    wantedBy = [ "multi-user.target" ];
    serviceConfig.Type = "oneshot";
    script = ''
      for f in /etc/systemd/network/10-cloud-init-*.network; do
        [ -e "$f" ] || continue
        [ -e "$f.d/10-tierhive-lldp.conf" ] && continue
        mkdir -p "$f.d"
        printf '[Network]\nLLDP=no\n' > "$f.d/10-tierhive-lldp.conf"
      done
    '';
  };

  systemd.services.tierhive-reserved-blocks = {
    description = "Set root fs reserved blocks to 0.01% after first-boot grow";
    wantedBy = [ "multi-user.target" ];
    after = [ "cloud-init.service" ];
    unitConfig.ConditionPathExists = "!/var/lib/tierhive/reserved-blocks.done";
    path = [ pkgs.e2fsprogs pkgs.util-linux pkgs.gawk ];
    serviceConfig.Type = "oneshot";
    script = ''
      dev=$(findmnt -no SOURCE /)
      blocks=$(tune2fs -l "$dev" | awk -F: '/^Block count/{gsub(/ /,"",$2); print $2}')
      tune2fs -r $((blocks / 10000)) "$dev"
      mkdir -p /var/lib/tierhive
      touch /var/lib/tierhive/reserved-blocks.done
    '';
  };
}
Have a play and a tweak and report back, maybe this can go further

I recently finished my second "novella".Don't even think about it: I won't publish this. Nor send it to you privately, which is mostly the same as publishing it.The first story is roughly 50 A5 pages long (~15k words), the second one is about 120 pages (~40k words). (Both barely fit the definition of a "novella", apparently, with the first one being a bit too short and the second one a tiny bit too long.)It feels pretty absurd and surreal that I have done this at all. In school, related to "writing" was pure horror for me -- unless it was related to maths, physics, or other "technical" stuff. But here we are.How long did it take? Well, the first one took over seven months. But the second one, the longer one? Ten days.Some time last year, probably around July, I started reading some of the books that were lying around in my appartment for decades. And this was much more enjoyable than I thought. No idea why. It's not like I never read a book before, but it was always a somewhat boring thing to do and it took me ages -- unless it was something technical. I'm only referring to fiction and novels here. Like, one book per year, tops. Yet, I somehow got hooked last year and now I've read about 30 novels since July 2025. Some faster (a couple of days), some slower (three months).It wasn't that much of a stretch to try to do this myself.This blog is still technical in nature, so I'll walk through the software that I used.Both of my books (they're not really "books", but I'll keep referring to them as such for the sake of simplicity) are typeset in groff. You know, the thing that usually renders manual pages in your terminal on Linux. groff/troff is a full typesetting system, though, and it can be used to produce real books, which I've already mentioned a couple of times in previous blog posts.In my case, the appeal of groff is that it's relatively small and , but still able to produce high-quality output. Making that 50-page document takes about on my 13.5 year old PC, and the 120-page one takes . This includes some pre-processing which we'll cover later. Given that most modern software these days feels like walking in mud, this is refreshingly quick.Because it's more fun that way, I made my own little macro package. There really isn't a lot to it, though. It's tiny. Not a lot is needed for a simple novel / novella -- no table of contents, no cross-references, no diagrams, no code blocks, no lists, ... Just nicely flowing text and a word gets emphasized.Disclaimer: You'll note some TODO items in the text below. groff is a full-blown typesetting system and these things are complicated. I'm a long way from having mastered every aspect of it.There are only three macros:.\" Section header / chapter title .de SH . sp 1v . ps +5p . ce \f[HR]\\$1\fP . ps . sp 4v .. .\" Normal paragraph with hanging indent. .de PP . br . ti +2m .. .\" "Long" paragraph, longer empty space. .de LP . sp 2v .. (The name is a spill-over from manual pages. I've written quite a few of those and this name somehow stuck. for "chapter heading" or for "chapter title" might have been better choices. Or, you know, .)You start defining a macro with -- anything until is part of that macro.: Go down one vee, increase type size, request centering, print the macro's first argument with the font (Helvetica), return to previous type size, add another four vees of space.: Just break the current line, then request a temporary indent for the next output line. This creates a hanging indent, which is customary in German books.: Just some empty space between paragraphs. Used for things like intra-chapter time jumps.You can look up the individual commands in .And that would already be enough to produce a document that somewhat resembles a book.Put the snippet above in a file called . I made one file per chapter. For example: . You can then make a PDF using a command like this:groff -Tpdf -dpaper=a5 -P-pa5 macros.groff chapter01.groff >book.pdf A5 is roughly the page size of a "typical book".You'll note that there are no page numbers by default. You could actually just decide to not have them if all you want is a digital PDF. They're useful for printing, but you don't really need them in a file, if we're being honest.Except: The thing gets confused about page breaks. Create the PDF using the example above and you'll see:Looks like the baseline of the last line of output is just outside the page. Not sure what's going on here -- it's related to setting the page size to A5. In other words, I probably missed spot where this has to be set. It'll be fixed implicitly by the following.The whole page number thing consists of three parts: First, how large is the footer (the area where the page numbers live)? I defined this in a register (which is not really necessary but I find the "code" easier to read this way) -- the name is arbitary:We than plant a trap at the bottom of the page:.wh -\n[_FooterSize]u _FooterTrap "gets" the value of the register called . There's a minus sign in front of it, it's negative and relative to the bottom of the page. The documentation says:A general-purpose register (one created or updated with the nr request) is implicitly dimensionless, or reckoned in basic units if interpreted in a measurement context.I'm not 100% sure why I need to add the . I haven't read every page of the manual, yet. Needless to say, it doesn't work without it.Okay, so the trap gets installed at 5v before the end of the page and it'll call the macro. It looks like this:.de _FooterTrap .\" Push current environment on stack and switch to `_FooterEnv`. .\" Parent env will keep partially collected lines. . ev _FooterEnv .\" Copy settings from default environment. . evc 0 .\" Don't do this for pages 1 and 2, they're title pages in my actual .\" "book". . if \\n[%]>2 \{\ .\" Set vertical position, explained in the text below. . sp |\\n[.p]u-2v .\" Print % register centered. . ce \fR\\n[%]\fP \} .\" Go back to previous environment. . ev .\" Request page break. ' bp .. It's much easier to read without the inline comments:.de _FooterTrap . ev _FooterEnv . evc 0 . if \\n[%]>2 \{\ . sp |\\n[.p]u-2v . ce \fR\\n[%]\fP \} . ev ' bp .. Why is it instead of ? We'll get to that.What I'm unsure about: I set the vertical position to . That's relative to the of the page, it's the page length minus two vee. I would have expected (including the "minus one vee" mentioned in the docs) to also work, but that leads to an infinite loop (probably triggers the same trap again and again). Why?For symmetry, I also want some empty space at the top of the page:.nr _HeaderSize 5v .de _HeaderTrap ' sp |\\n[_HeaderSize]u .. .wh 0v _HeaderTrap And another TODO for me: This isn't 100% symmetrical. The length of the header is a bit larger than the footer. An issue with the page size again?Let's have a closer look at traps. I'm not sure on this -- this is to the best of my understanding.Actually, let's take a step back from the example above. Let's start with this:This is a little excerpt from Mary Shelly's Frankenstein.
These visions faded when I perused, for the first time, those poets whose effusions entranced my soul and lifted it to heaven. I also became a poet and for one year lived in a paradise of my own creation; I imagined that I also might obtain a niche in the temple where the names of Homer and Shakespeare are consecrated. You are well acquainted with my failure and how heavily I bore the disappointment. But just at that time I inherited the fortune of my cousin, and my thoughts were turned into the channel of their earlier bent.
Six years have passed since I resolved on my present undertaking. I can, even now, remember the hour from which I dedicated myself to this great enterprise. I commenced by inuring my body to hardship. I accompanied the whale-fishers on several expeditions to the North Sea; I voluntarily endured cold, famine, thirst, and want of sleep; I often worked harder than the common sailors during the day and devoted my nights to the study of mathematics, the theory of medicine, and those branches of physical science from which a naval adventurer might derive the greatest practical advantage. Twice I actually hired myself as an under-mate in a Greenland whaler, and acquitted myself to admiration. I must own I felt a little proud when my captain offered me the second dignity in the vessel and entreated me to remain with the greatest earnestness, so valuable did he consider my services. Save it as and make a PDF:groff -Tpdf test.groff >book.pdf Install a trap at 10v. It's empty so far and does nothing -- insert this at the very top of the file:.de MyTrap .. .wh 10v MyTrap .\" Now what happens when we change the trap macro to this?When the output reached the word "fishers", it sprang the trap. "FOO" comes after "fishers" in the PDF: "fishers" triggered the trap, then "FOO" got inserted. I think it's as simple as that, isn't it?Suppose we want to put "FOO" at the beginning of the line and we want nothing but that word on that line. "fishers" shall be put on the next line.If I understand correctly, switching environments is key: The environment holds the currently collected line (among many other things). Switch to a new one and we get a new buffer/line -- one that's empty so far. We can then flush/break this line to have it included in the PDF, and then switch back to the previous environment:.de MyTrap . ev MyTrapEnv FOO . br . ev .. So far, so good. Now I want there to be an empty line before and after "FOO":.de MyTrap . ev MyTrapEnv . sp FOO . sp . ev .. In the new environment, we issue : It breaks and inserts space. Nothing was collected so far, so the break doesn't print anything at this point. Then we have -- this isn't printed directly, it's just collected. The next breaks again (thus printing "FOO") and then inserts more space.Okay. Now. Suppose that printing "FOO" with those two lines around it is all we want to do inside of the trap. We then want the normal text to continue at some absolute position on the page, say at 17v.So, how about the following? We go back to the previous environment and then issue an absolute location request:.de MyTrap . ev MyTrapEnv . sp FOO . sp . ev . sp |17v .. broke the already collected line (the one with just "fishers" in it) and inserted the space. We don't want that break. (Also note that "fishers" is at the exact same position where we left off inside the trap.)This can be avoided by using instead of :' The neutral apostrophe is the no-break control character, rec‐ ognized where the control character is. It suppresses the (first) break implied by the bp, ce, cf, fi, fl, "in", nf, rj, sp, ti, and trf requests. The requested operation takes ef‐ fect at the next break. .de MyTrap . ev MyTrapEnv . sp FOO . sp . ev ' sp |17v .. The example in the documentation doesn't change environments. But I find it much easier to understand what's going when changing them. (And I hope I got it right.)One final remark: Environment carry settings. starts a completely new environment (unless an environment with that name already exists), it doesn't clone the previous one. That's why my macro package also does .As a little exercise, see what happens when you use , , , all the way down to .Only when writing this blog post, I found the following sentence in the docs (Basics):If you don't start a sentence on a new line, put two spaces after the previous sentence. GNU troff then recognizes punctuation that ends a sentence, and inserts inter-sentence space accordingly.I didn't do that, because this isn't customary in German. But now I see it. Sometimes, it looks like this in the PDF:And sometimes there's a little bit of extra space:As far as I know, this is usually not done in German books. I disabled it with this request, which keeps "minimal inter-word spacing" at the default value of 12 but sets "supplemental inter-sentence spacing" to 0:This isn't part of my macro package, but it comes first in the "index" file (, see demo package below).Apparently, it's as simple as this: is "de(utsch) new": This includes the "new" German rules from 2006.Markdown and other pre-processingI actually wrote both of my stories in plain groff, using the macros I explained above.Markdown has the big advantage of being "visually pleasing": It mostly looks like text that is intended to be read by a human being. groff isn't, really. I mean, it wasn't , I wouldn't have written over 170 pages in groff if this was . It's fine. I use groff in several other places as well. But Markdown is a tiny little bit better, and I assume that it allows me to focus more on the creative process.So, afterwards, I converted the second story to "Markdown". Didn't bother to do that for the first one. I put that in quotation marks because there are only very few elements, it's not full Markdown: at the top of the file.Empty lines between paragraphs, for "long empty paragraphs". for emphasized text.And that's it. Makes the conversion really easy.I could have just use plain Markdown for this whole thing and created an HTML file. I already have the tooling for that -- that's what drives my blog. Maybe this could even produce a PDF file. But what would be missing there, among other things, would be an engine to . It hurts my eyes to see justified text in HTML, it just looks like a complete disaster. The results that groff or TeX create are much, much better.While we're at it, we can also do some basic character replacements: I have the German typographic quotation marks U+201E and U+201C directly on my keyboard, but Guillemets are preferred in books.I first wanted to do all this in Python but then switched to a little Perl snippet, because it makes it easy to read the entire file and then do multiline-regex stuff on it. It goes something like this:perl -0pe ' # Guillemets are preferred in books. s/... German typographic quotes, see demo package .../... Guillemets .../g;
# Markdown "h1" into ".SH". s/^# ([^\n]+)\n/.SH "\1"\n/smg;
# Markdown "hr" into ".LP". s/\n---\n/.LP/smg;
# Markdown paragraphs into ".PP". s/\n\n/\n.PP\n/smg;
# Markdown emphasize into groff font selection: s/\*([^*]+)\*/\\fI\1\\fP/smg; ' The output is still groff for my little macro package. But I can now write it like this:# Kapitel 42: Die Antwort
Da war sie also. Die Antwort auf *alle* Fragen.
Und dann ging es weiter:
Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Vestibulum hendrerit blandit suscipit. Maecenas sed nisi nec ante mattis vulputate. Pellentesque maximus leo id purus vestibulum dictum. Donec tincidunt mollis viverra. Curabitur at ligula in turpis pellentesque vehicula.
Duis hendrerit commodo mauris, non posuere leo vulputate ut. Nulla finibus urna eget vehicula auctor. Mauris in posuere nibh. Vivamus ut metus vel nisi gravida molestie vitae et tellus. Integer mi odio, vestibulum eget laoreet vel, auctor nec ligula. Quisque eu placerat quam, eu tincidunt mauris. Nam vestibulum lectus non leo dignissim, non sagittis dolor venenatis. This reads much nicer and there is syntax highlighting in Vim for it. Well, the parts, that is -- the rest doesn't matter:There is one super important Vim plugin for this:It hides almost all UI elements.On top of that, I put the terminal window in fullscreen mode to hide my desktop.And there you go. "Distraction-free writing" indeed. Maybe put on some music, but there's nothing else. No browser, no clock, no nothing. Just a typewriter with an easy undo function.The font is the 11x22 variant of UW ttyp0.There's a popular way to format text in plain-text files:This is one sentence. This is another one. And so on and so on. One sentence per line, that's it. Sentences can be short. Or they can be pretty long, sometimes even exceeding the available width on your screen, thus requiring you to either scroll or have your editor soft-wrap it for you when displaying it. As we've learned above from the groff docs, this actually carries meaning for groff (properly detect the ends of sentences). And it makes the diffs in VCS's less noisy.Another popular way would be to put each paragraph on its own line. Like, there's only one "physical" newline character after the whole thing and it's your editor's job to properly display this:This is one sentence. This is another one. And so on and so on. One sentence per line, that's it. Sentences can be short. Or they can be pretty long, sometimes even exceeding the available width on your screen, thus requiring you to either scroll or have your editor soft-wrap it for you when displaying it. I used the second version for a couple of years at the end of the 1990ies and the early 2000s, but eventually switched to hard-wrapped text. Like this:This is one sentence. This is another one. And so on and so on. One sentence per line, that's it. Sentences can be short. Or they can be pretty long, sometimes even exceeding the available width on your screen, thus requiring you to either scroll or have your editor soft-wrap it for you when displaying it. I just find this easier to read and easier to navigate in. And it reminds me of the "good" old DOS days, where I used the editor a lot and all my files looked like that (manually formatted, of course). It does have drawbacks, yes. It's not great for collaboration with other people, but that doesn't matter in my case.Another drawback is that I have to reformat these paragraphs while editing. Luckily, Vim makes that easy:" In ~/.vimrc: set nojs map Q gq} I can then just put my cursor at the beginning of a paragraph:[T]his is one sentence. This is another one. And so on and so on. One sentence per line, that's it. Sentences can be short. And hit to turn it into this:This is one sentence. This is another one. And so on and so on. One sentence per line, that's it. Sentences can be short. [_] The cursor will be positioned after that paragraph. To reflow the entire document, I can just keep pressed down until it hits the end.-- edit: Or do to have paragraphs reformatted automatically while editing it. I usually don't want that, I usually want it to be an explicit operation. But for writing prose, this might be pretty useful.The option (funny name, huh?) was new to me, only found out about it just now. The full name is and it prevents the very thing that I be doing (but which is not customary in German): It does insert two spaces after a period. Without it, i.e., with , the result would look like this:This is one sentence. This is another one. And so on and so on. One sentence per line, that's it. Sentences can be short. Some people like that, I don't.As with virtually everything I do, this is in Git.But that's the end. I regularly commit the current state, but I hardly care about anything else. No proper commit messages, nothing. It's just a tool for making "backups" easier and to sync the data between my laptop and desktop PC.: Putting it all togetherAs I said, I won't publish the actual "books". But here's a demo of the "scaffold":(I won't update this. This is just a snapshot of the current status.)The writing process itselfI won't say much about this. I'm a total noob.I changed course a couple of times. For both stories, I tried to draft the whole thing before I began writing the actual text, but that didn't help much. The first story isn't that great anyway. It has an interesting premise, but I wasn't really "in the zone". That's also why it took so long. The second story has a much more mundane basic idea to it and then I eventually realized how interesting it actually was. There are quite a few twists and turns in it, and I love that.It was harder than I thought to keep the facts straight, especially with the course changes. Which character knows what, when, why? What are the motives? Is everything consistent and plausible? Constantly question everything -- does she need to do that now or is there some other, much more likely way out? Does every sentence she said 100 pages ago still make sense after the big reveal at the end? Stuff like that. I'll have to get better at that. Probably use a little "character inventory" next time, like, a profile for each one.Building a world with its own set of rules and history and what not turns out to be very fascinating. As is building the characters.The length of the stories make proof-reading ridiculously hard. Like, I'm not going to read 120 pages in a couple of minutes. I re-read the second story one last time yesterday and it took ... *checks notes* ... three hours. Ouch.Now that I've re-read that 120-page thing again yesterday ... I actually like it. I'm happy with the result. There's obviously a lot to learn: This entire field is completely new to me. I have identified quite a few places where the storytelling isn't great -- the pace isn't right, things progress too fast. (At the moment, I only notice this when reading the whole thing in one go. Oof.) Sometimes the wording isn't great. Not enough detail. And so on. But the story itself is something that I'd enjoy reading myself.Digging deeper into groff is on my TODO list, also for obvious reasons.What I really loved about this whole thing is that I ended up completely in the tunnel. On one of those ten days that it took me to write the 120-page thing, I wasn't even actually typing on a keyboard: We were travelling across the country, lots of idle time, I didn't have a laptop, but I basically "wrote" at least one chapter and then memorized (most of) it. Went back and forth, new ideas. Completely absorbed.Maybe stuff will eventually get published (as in: I might put it on this website), but not now. I do this for myself. I'm not even remotely interested in my work getting torn apart by rude critics. Leave me alone. :-)

When you see your databases CPU at 100%, what is it  doing? We like to act as though CPUs run code linearly for simplicity's sake, but in truth CPUs are incredibly complex beasts; they can potentially do many instructions at once, or sometimes none at all (despite our console showing 100%). Because CPUs can do so many things, one CPU at 100% may be doing much less than another at 100%. A CPU is considered "idle" only if no task (Linux task) needs CPU time; this does not mean the CPU itself is being fully saturated. So why would a CPU not be fully utilized, even if it appears to be at 100%?The answer is usually .Memory accesses are orders of magnitude slower than internal CPU operations. To give a good picture of how much so, a modern CPU might be able to process somewhere in the realm of 4-8 instructions per nanosecond. A memory access to RAM can take somewhere in the realm of 50-100ns. To ease your mathing, that means a modern CPU can do around 400-800 instructions for every  memory access from RAM.~64 KB · ~4–8 instructions per access~1 MB · ~15–30 instructions per accessup to a few hundred MB · ~50–100 instructions per accessGBs · ~400–800 instructions per accessGiven this, CPUs are built from the ground up to work their way around this. The most obvious way this is dealt with is CPU caches. CPU caches keep memory closer to the CPU than RAM, literally baked into the CPU chip. The closest cache (the L1 cache) takes around 1ns per memory operation- that's 50-100x faster than RAM! The "problem" here of course is that the CPU caches are fairly small; the L1 cache for a modern CPU is probably around ~64KB, while the farther caches max out at a few hundred MB for top end processors. A good database tries manipulating data in small "chunks" to take full advantage of caches, and you'll often hear cache utilization as being one of the top concerns of performance engineering.Let's look at GROUP BY aggregations to get a better grip on cache utilization. In GROUP BY, we're looking to take a dataset, group it by a certain column (or more), and perform certain aggregations (for example, count, or sum on another column). The "naive" method might be to open a HashTable keyed by the group key that grows ever larger. For every new row, you would check if that key exists- if it does, go to it's corresponding aggregation and update the result. Simple, right? The problem is that if you have millions of unique groups, you're not utilizing your cache at all! Nearly every HashTable access will be a "cold" hit that goes to RAM. This means that although your CPU may /look/ like it's 100% utilized, in truth it'll constantly be waiting for cold memory accesses.To demonstrate this, we'll take our state-of-the-art Pivot engine and ravage it! We'll remove all our complex logic and replace it with simplicity:(
    &,
    batch: ,
    _sender: &<>,
) -> unary::<()> {
     group_keys = batch.(.key_column).::<>();
     &key  group_keys.() {

        *.counts.(key).() += ;
    }
}We'll then run a simple query SELECT count(*), UserID FROM hits GROUP BY UserID on the "hits" dataset ClickBench provides (99m rows). This query will give us a count per , meaning we'll have an entry in our HashMap per - I'll give a spoiler here that there are ~17m unique .
Our performance checks will be on a bare metal server from our good friends over at CherryServers (AMD EPYC 9124 Genoa, 16 cores, 128 GB DDR5). We unfortunately can't use a common AWS/GCP machine because we want access to counters that aren't exposed there for security reasons (for example, we want to see how much memory is passing from DRAM controller to us). We'll confine ourselves to a single core, since this naive group by can't run on many cores. > config.yaml <<:
  : 127.0.0.1:5440
: 1
:
  :
    : pivotlake
    : ./data
    : true
:
  :
    :
      : trust
 3  server  config.yaml &
 7  127.0.0.1  5440  pivot This takes... a whopping 3.9 seconds. Is that good? Is that bad? Everything in this world is relative, or so they say. If we run the query again (without restarting the server), it takes 3.6s. The second run is the one we're more interested in- there's no disk involved as everything is already sitting in our page-cache.You'll notice we did . This pins the process to a given CPU; this will allow us to run  on that CPU and see what's going on internally. We'll measure two metric groups- percentage of CPU slots stalling because of memory (and not doing anything in the meanwhile), and percentage of CPU slots actually retiring a micro-op: stat  3  backend_bound_memory,retiring --  7  127.0.0.1  5440  pivot Performance counter stats for 'CPU(s) 3':

        8364109137      ex_no_retire.load_not_complete   #     50.1 %  backend_bound_memory
       38233214040      de_no_dispatch_per_slot.backend_stalls
        8977647849      ex_no_retire.not_complete
       11861387798      ls_not_halted_cyc
       11431618219      ex_ret_ops                       #     16.1 %  retiring
       11858248045      ls_not_halted_cyc

       4.030148989 seconds time elapsedThis tells us that 50.1% of the time, the CPU couldn't do anything because it was stalling on memory! And only 16.1% of the time the CPU was actually retiring (aka doing) operations.Another interesting stat to get is how many of our CPU memory loads are coming from DRAM (and not from caches): stat  3    ls_dmnd_fills_from_sys.dram_io_near   --  7  127.0.0.1  5440  pivot  Performance counter stats for 'CPU(s) 3':

          76190837      ls_dmnd_fills_from_sys.dram_io_near

       3.996301634 seconds time elapsedEvery load is 64 bytes (the minimum amount you can bring, a cache line), so we can multiply by 64 to see we pulled 4.5GB during this query from DRAM on CPU loads (this is without any prefetching).Let's now try to improve our algorithm to take advantage of the CPU caches. To do this, we'll use a method called radix partitioning. The idea is to split the merge into two distinct phases: In this phase, we're simply going to separate the keys into 4096 different partitions (simply a Vec of SlabVecs- a SlabVec is a data structure similar to a Vec that is above memory that is already paged in. We don't use Vecs for raw memory in Pivot because it creates a lot of variance; not something for this blog post!). The partition will be decided by hashing the key and taking it's top 12 bits (2**12=4096).(
    &,
    batch: ,
    _sender: &<>,
) -> unary::<()> {
     keys = batch.(.key_column).::<>();
     &key  keys.() {
         hash = .hasher.(key);
         partition = (hash >> )  usize;
        .partitions[partition].(&.allocator, (hash, key));
    }
    (())
}partition = , the first 3 hex digits When all data has come through, go partition by partition, each time creating a HashMap, aggregating the partition into it, and then outputting it. This phase is very similar to our original algorithm, but we're going to use the hash from phase 1.() -> unary::<<::>> {
     groups = ::();
     rows  &.partitions {
         table: <(u64, i64, u64)> = ::();   
        rows.(|(hash, key)| {
             table.(hash, |group| group. == key, |group| group.) {
                ::( group) => group.(). += ,
                ::(slot) => { slot.((hash, key, )); }
            }
        });
        groups.(table.().(|(_, key, count)| (key, count)));
    }
    ((::(groups)))
}Partitions, one at a timeAt first glance, this solution may seem rather odd. We're doing  operations here, not less! Every row, in both solutions, is hashed and sent into a HashMap. But in this solution, we're first pushing rows into seemingly meaningless vecs.The key here (no pun intended) is to understand why we've done this; it's to make the aggregating HashMaps . By dividing our group by into 4096 pieces, we've made our working HashMaps much likelier to fit into our CPU caches. Every time we do a lookup on our HashMap, our CPU is more likely to find the relevant memory sitting in it's caches, which saves a big chunk of time. But why trust me? Let's run it! 7  127.0.0.1  5440  pivot And... Boom! 3.3 seconds, and then on second run 2.7 seconds! We've definitely made an improvement. But to really drive this point home, let's run perf again: stat  3  backend_bound_memory,retiring --  7  127.0.0.1  5440  pivot  Performance counter stats for 'CPU(s) 3':

        3790955223      ex_no_retire.load_not_complete   #     23.5 %  backend_bound_memory
       20376118172      de_no_dispatch_per_slot.backend_stalls
        5442424751      ex_no_retire.not_complete
       10083617764      ls_not_halted_cyc
       19350459182      ex_ret_ops                       #     32.2 %  retiring
       10023435553      ls_not_halted_cyc

       3.575992317 seconds time elapsedNow we can see the picture has completely changed- 23.5% of the time is spent on waiting for memory (as opposed to 50% before), 32% for actually retiring operations. You may notice we're actually doing  work, not less- in this run, we did 19350459182 operations (the  counter), whereas before the database did 11431618219 operations. This is worth it though, because as we said before- if we can translate cold DRAM accesses into CPU instructions, it's nearly always a good trade to make. In this case, we went down from 8364109137 CPU slots stalled because of memory to 3790955223.To complete the picture and really see we're pulling less memory from the DRAM, let's again check how many our memory loads come from DRAM: stat  3    ls_dmnd_fills_from_sys.dram_io_near   --  7  127.0.0.1  5440  pivot  Performance counter stats for 'CPU(s) 3':

          25688280      ls_dmnd_fills_from_sys.dram_io_near

       3.009547012 seconds time elapsedWhoo! That means that on demand we pulled 1.5GB from DRAM, instead of 4.5GB in the naive version.That, of course, isn't the limit of how much we can utilize our CPU cache for GROUP BYs. And utilizing our CPU cache isn't the only way we can "hide" DRAM latency; another trick is prefetching memory to our cache before we need it, and yet another trick is creating algorithms that allow maximization of memory throughput- but that's not for this blog! We'll go into that in our next blog where we look at our /seemingly very weird/ implementation of JOIN probing.For now, without explaining too much, I'll leave you with Pivot's speed for this query on a single core:Time: 1848.353 ms (00:01.848)and the amount of RAM we bring: Performance counter stats for 'CPU(s) 3':

          14917958      ls_dmnd_fills_from_sys.dram_io_near

       2.182674864 seconds time elapsedWhich comes out to 954 MB.Of course, Pivot knows to run in parallel on our 16 cores (where there are many other interesting cases), in which case it comes out to:Try out Pivot if you want to have super speeds above parquets (OSS fun!), see you next time.
